= Metaphysics — Simple =

'''Metaphysics''' is the branch of philosophy that asks very general questions about reality.

Examples include:

* What exists?
* What kinds of things exist?
* What makes something the same thing over time?
* What is possibility?
* What is causation?
* What makes one fact more fundamental than another?

Metaphysics is **not** simply another word for supernatural or mystical beliefs.

Modern metaphysics often works closely with logic and science.<ref name="sep">[https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/metaphysics/ Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Metaphysics.”]</ref>

== Ontology ==

'''Ontology''' asks what exists.

Possible questions include:

* Do numbers exist?
* Are properties real?
* Are events things?
* Do social institutions exist?
* Are wholes more than their parts?

Different philosophers give different answers.

== Identity ==

Suppose a ship slowly replaces every plank.

Is it still the same ship?

Questions like this study '''identity through change'''.

People also change physically throughout life.

Metaphysics asks what makes an entity persist as the same entity.

== Parts and wholes ==

'''Mereology''' studies parts and wholes.

Questions include:

* When do several objects make one larger object?
* Can two objects share all the same matter?
* Does every collection of things form a whole?

These sound simple but produce difficult philosophical problems.

== Possibility and necessity ==

'''Modality''' studies what is possible and necessary.

“2 + 2 = 4” is usually treated as necessary.

“A tree is in this exact location” is contingent: it could have been otherwise.

Modal logic uses symbols such as:

<math>\Box p</math> — necessarily <math>p</math>;

<math>\Diamond p</math> — possibly <math>p</math>.

== Possible worlds ==

Philosophers sometimes talk about '''possible worlds'''.

A possible world is a way reality could have been.

Different theories disagree about what possible worlds are.

Some treat them as abstract representations.

David Lewis famously argued that possible worlds are concrete realities.

Using possible-world language does not require accepting Lewis's theory.

== Causation ==

Metaphysics asks what it means for one thing to cause another.

Different theories emphasize:

* regular patterns;
* counterfactual dependence;
* interventions;
* causal powers;
* physical processes.

Science can discover causal relations.

Metaphysics asks what kind of relation causation itself is.

== Time ==

Questions about time include:

* Does only the present exist?
* Are the past and future also real?
* Does time really “flow”?

Major views include:

'''Presentism''' — only the present exists.

'''Eternalism''' — past, present, and future are equally real.

Physics strongly constrains these debates, but philosophers still disagree about interpretation.

== Grounding ==

'''Grounding''' is a proposed relation of metaphysical dependence.

For example, a fact about a committee may depend on facts about its members and rules.

A higher-level fact can be real while depending on more basic facts.

Grounding is meant to explain this kind of dependence.<ref name="grounding">[https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/grounding/ Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Metaphysical Grounding.”]</ref>

== Universals ==

Suppose two apples are both red.

Why can they share one property?

One answer says there is a universal property called redness.

Another says only individual red things exist.

This is the debate between realism about universals and nominalist alternatives.

== Social reality ==

Money, corporations, laws, and offices depend on human practices.

They are still real in the sense that they affect behavior and institutions.

“Socially constructed” does not mean imaginary.

It means the entity depends on social rules or practices.

== Metaphysics and science ==

Science can constrain metaphysics.

For example:

* relativity matters for theories of time;
* quantum physics matters for identity and locality;
* evolution matters for theories of biological kinds.

Metaphysics should not ignore established scientific evidence.

But scientific equations do not always decide every philosophical interpretation.

== Thought experiments ==

Philosophers often use thought experiments.

These can help test whether a theory is consistent with our concepts.

Thought experiments are not substitutes for real experiments when the question is empirical.

== Metametaphysics ==

'''Metametaphysics''' asks whether metaphysical questions are genuine, how they should be answered, and whether some disputes are partly verbal.

So philosophers disagree not only about reality but also about how metaphysics should be done.

